course: adult-life
lesson: 49
title: How to handle gossip: is it true, is it kind, would I say it to their face
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Gossip is not only about famous people; most of it is about the people right next to you, in the lunch queue, the group chat and the playground. And there it hurts most, because the person has to walk into class the next morning.
explain: A rumour grows each time it is passed on: a spilled water bottle becomes "she wet herself", "he's away working" becomes "he's in prison", and Lily said Maya said Zoe said becomes something Hannah never said.
explain: Vivid example: in Toronto, Mum is on the phone with her friend: "And you'll never guess what Karen's — actually, no. I shouldn't say. It's not my story to tell." She hangs up and winks at you: "Best sentence I know, that one."
explain: Three questions stop most cruel gossip: is it true, is it kind, is it needed? And one more: would I say it to their face?
fact: Rumours about classmates are cruel, especially about their bodies; you can stop one by not passing it on.
fact: A classmate's family, like where a dad works or why parents are sad, is private; don't pass on what you heard.
fact: Explaining away someone's good marks as "copying" is usually jealousy dressed as news.
fact: Sharing someone's photo to laugh at them, without asking, is not fair; pictures of a person belong to them.
fact: "It's not my story to tell" means keeping a friend's news for the friend to share.
fact: Before passing something on, ask: is it true, is it kind, is it needed?
fact: If you would not say it to their face, don't say it behind their back.
fact: Chains of "she said" twist the truth; ask the person yourself.
fact: Enjoying a silly, obviously made-up story is fine, as long as everyone knows it is silly; friendly chat that keeps sad things private is fine too.
fact: Online is not the same as true: check where a story comes from and its date, and look for two trusted sources before sharing news; if you are not sure, "I don't know" is a fine answer.
fact: Never accuse someone because "everyone says"; accusations are for a teacher to look into.
fact: A secret told to you is a gift; giving it away hurts the friend who trusted you.
fact: "I'm just saying what I heard" and "I only told one person" are still spreading a rumour.
fact: Sharing a cruel rumour online can count as bullying, even if you didn't write it, and people who gossip about everyone end up trusted by nobody.
exercise: A girl in the lunch queue: "Ellie wet herself on the trip, tell everyone!" Really, Ellie spilled her water bottle. What do you do? | Don't pass it on; it's cruel and untrue | Tell everyone, it's a funny story | Ask Ellie in front of the class | Tell just your best friends, quietly, at break time
exercise: A boy at football: "Josh's dad's in prison, my mum said." Josh's dad works on oil rigs. How did the rumour start? | Someone misheard "he's away" | Josh told everyone himself | The police announced it | Josh's dad said it as a joke
exercise: Whispers: "Amira copied, that's why she got full marks." What is it most likely? | Jealousy dressed up as news | A fact the teacher has proved | A kind thing to say about Amira | Something Amira admitted herself
exercise: The group chat: "Look at Ben's baby pic lol, send it round." What should you do? | Don't share someone's photo to laugh at them | Send it, baby photos are always fine to share with anyone | Add a funny caption then share it | Only share it with people Ben knows
exercise: Mum stops on the phone: "Actually, it's not my story to tell." What does she mean? | It's her friend's news to share, not hers | She has forgotten what happened | The story is far too boring to tell to anyone | She'll tell it tomorrow instead
exercise: Grandad: "Before you pass that on, ask yourself three things." What are they? | Is it true, is it kind, is it needed? | Is it new, is it funny, is it short? | Is it secret, is it loud, is it big? | Is it mine, is it yours, is it theirs?
exercise: The teacher says, "Would you say it to their face?" What is the rule? | If not, don't say it behind their back | If not, write it down in a note instead | If not, say it twice as loudly | If not, ask a friend to say it
exercise: "Lily said Maya said Zoe said Hannah hates you." What is the best thing to do? | Ask Hannah yourself | Stop being Hannah's friend | Tell Lily to tell Hannah off | Believe it, four people said so
exercise: Big sister: "I read it online so it must be true." What's right? | Online is not the same as true; check the source | Everything online is checked by experts before it goes up | Nothing online is ever true at all | It's true if a lot of people liked it
exercise: Dad laughs at a headline: "ALIEN BABY BORN IN KENT." Is it okay to enjoy it? | Yes, as long as you know it's nonsense | No, newspapers are never meant to be funny | Yes, because it is probably true | No, aliens might be upset by it
exercise: Nan loves "a bit of gossip with my tea, not the nasty sort". What keeps gossip okay? | Keeping it kind and keeping sad things private | Only gossiping when you have a cup of tea | Making the stories as shocking as you can | Only gossiping about people far away
exercise: "Everyone says Ryan stole the class money." What is really known? | The money is missing; nobody knows who took it | Ryan took it, because everyone in the class says so | Ryan has owned up to the teacher | The teacher saw Ryan take it
exercise: A girl whispers, "Promise you won't tell, but Jess fancies Tom." What should you do? | Keep it; Jess trusted her friend with it | Tell Tom so he knows | Tell the class, it's sweet | Tell Jess you're going to tell Tom anyway
exercise: Big brother: "I'm not gossiping, I'm just saying what I heard." Is he right? | No, passing on a rumour is still spreading it | Yes, only the person who starts it is really gossiping | Yes, if he heard it from a grown-up | No, because he should have made it up
exercise: A girl says, "I only told one person." That person told five. What is the lesson? | "Only one person" is how every rumour spreads | Telling one person never matters at all | Five people is not very many | She should have told nobody but two
exercise: Kids say Sophie's parents are divorcing because she was crying. What should you do? | Ask Sophie if she's okay, don't make up why | Tell everyone so they're nice to her | Ask her loudly if it's true | Ask her parents what is going on at home
exercise: You come home upset because kids are gossiping about you. Mum says, "It says more about them than you." What does she mean? | Cruel gossip shows the gossiper's own unkindness | The gossip about you must be true then | You should gossip about them right back, only worse | Only bad people ever get gossiped about
exercise: The teacher says, "Before you share a story, look for two trusted sources." What does that mean? | Check two different trusted places say the same | Ask two friends if they have heard it too | Share it twice so more people can check it for you | Read the same post two times carefully
test: Your mate in the chat: "omg everyone's sayin Ryan nicked the money, he's such a thief" You know nobody saw anything. Best reply? | We don't know who took it, leave it to Miss | Yeah he totally did, send it round | Let's search his bag at break | Tell his mum he's a thief
test: Group chat: "ben's baby pic lmaooo send it to the whole year 😂" Ben didn't agree. What is fair? | Don't share it, it's his picture to share | Share it, he was only a baby then | Share it, but delete it again after a day or two | Share it only if it's really funny
test: Big brother, grinning: "It's not gossip, I'm just repeating what Kev said, innit." Put him right. | Repeating a rumour is spreading it | He's right, only Kev is gossiping | It's fine if Kev is his mate | It's fine if he says it quietly
test: Your friend: "I only told Mia, swear!" Now the whole class knows Jess's secret. What happened? | Telling one person is how it spread | Mia must have read Jess's diary | Jess told everyone herself | Secrets always come out on their own
test: In the playground: "Sophie was crying, her mum and dad must be splitting up." Which is true? | Crying is a fact; the reason is a guess | The divorce is a fact everyone can see | Sophie's tears prove her parents argue | Crying always means a family problem
test: The teacher: "Liking and sharing a nasty post about someone counts too." What is she saying? | Sharing a cruel rumour online can be bullying | Only writing a post yourself can ever be bullying | Liking a post is always kind | Posts online don't count at school
test: Nan, stirring her tea: "There was a woman on our street who started every rumour. Nobody trusted her in the end." What happened to her? | She lost people's trust and friendship | She became the most popular woman there | She got a job at the newspaper | Everybody thanked her for the news
test: A boy, laughing at a classmate's old trainers: "But it's FUNNY!" The classmate isn't laughing. What is it? | Teasing, not a joke, if he isn't laughing | A joke, as long as most people laugh | Fine, because trainers aren't private | Fine, because it is only words
test: A girl at the gate: "Is it true about Mr Green getting sacked? You must know!" You've no idea. What's the best answer? | "I don't know" — that's allowed | Say yes, so you don't look silly | Make up a reason that sounds right | Say no, and invent what really happened
test: Kids whisper, "Josh's dad's in prison!" Josh comes into class. Who gets hurt by the rumour? | Josh, who now has to explain his family | Nobody, because Josh wasn't there | The kids who started it | Josh's dad, who is on an oil rig and won't hear
test: Mum is about to tell her friend some news, then stops: "Not mine to tell." What does she show you? | Keeping someone's news for them to share | Forgetting something halfway through | Being rude to her friend on the phone | That the news was a lie all along
test: Your mate: "Amira got full marks AGAIN, bet she copies." You saw her revising every lunchtime. What's the fair reply? | She worked hard, be glad for her | Yeah, let's tell Miss she copies | Ask her to give you her answers | Copy her homework yourself next time
exam: Identify the three questions which the lesson recommends be asked prior to the repetition of any account concerning another person. | Whether it is true, whether it is kind, and whether it is needed | Whether it is new, whether it is amusing, and whether it is brief | Whether it is secret, whether it is shocking, and whether it is popular | Whether it is written, whether it is signed, and whether it is dated
exam: Whereas a pupil asserts that he is "merely repeating what he heard", determine the correct characterisation of his conduct. | He is participating in the spreading of the rumour | He bears no responsibility whatsoever for the rumour | He is reporting a verified fact to his peers | He is the sole originator of the rumour
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning an assertion transmitted through a succession of four intermediaries. | It is likely to have been altered at each stage of transmission | It is rendered more reliable by each additional intermediary through whom it passes | It must be accepted once four persons have repeated it | It may be verified only by the final intermediary
exam: Ascertain the course of action which the lesson commends in respect of a secret confided by a classmate concerning her affections. | It ought to be kept, out of respect for the trust shown | It ought to be disclosed to the person concerned | It ought to be announced to the entire class | It ought to be exchanged for another secret
